#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:2
msgid "The Linux Kernel threat model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:4
msgid ""
"There are a lot of assumptions regarding what the kernel does and does not "
"protect against. These assumptions tend to cause confusion for bug reports (:"
"doc:`security-related ones <security-bugs>` vs :doc:`non-security ones <../"
"admin-guide/reporting-issues>`), and can complicate security enforcement "
"when the responsibilities for some boundaries is not clear between the "
"kernel, distros, administrators and users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:11
msgid ""
"This document tries to clarify the responsibilities of the kernel in this "
"domain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:15
msgid "The kernel's responsibilities"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:17
msgid ""
"The kernel abstracts access to local hardware resources and to remote "
"systems in a way that allows multiple local users to get a fair share of the "
"available resources granted to them, and, when the underlying hardware "
"permits, to assign a level of confidentiality to their communications and to "
"the data they are processing or storing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:23
msgid ""
"The kernel assumes that the underlying hardware behaves according to its "
"specifications. This includes the integrity of the CPU's instruction set, "
"the transparency of the branch prediction unit and the cache units, the "
"consistency of the Memory Management Unit (MMU), the isolation of DMA-"
"capable peripherals (e.g., via IOMMU), state transitions in controllers, "
"ranges of values read from registers, the respect of documented hardware "
"limitations, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:30
msgid ""
"When hardware fails to maintain its specified isolation (e.g., CPU bugs, "
"side-channels, hardware response to unexpected inputs), the kernel will "
"usually attempt to implement reasonable mitigations. These are best-effort "
"measures intended to reduce the attack surface or elevate the cost of an "
"attack within the limits of the hardware's facilities; they do not "
"constitute a kernel-provided safety guarantee."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:37
msgid ""
"Users always perform their activities under the authority of an "
"administrator who is able to grant or deny various types of permissions that "
"may affect how users benefit from available resources, or the level of "
"confidentiality of their activities. Administrators may also delegate all or "
"part of their own permissions to some users, particularly via capabilities "
"but not only. All this is performed via configuration (sysctl, file-system "
"permissions etc)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:44
msgid ""
"The Linux Kernel applies a certain collection of default settings that match "
"its threat model. Distros have their own threat model and will come with "
"their own configuration presets, that the administrator may have to adjust "
"to better suit their expectations (relax or restrict)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:49
msgid ""
"By default, the Linux Kernel guarantees the following protections when "
"running on common processors featuring privilege levels and memory "
"management units:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:52
msgid ""
"**User-based isolation**: an unprivileged user may restrict access to their "
"own data from other unprivileged users running on the same system. This "
"includes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:56
msgid "stored data, via file system permissions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:57
msgid "in-memory data (pages are not accessible by default to other users)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:58
msgid "process activity (ptrace is not permitted to other users)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:59
msgid ""
"inter-process communication (other users may not observe data exchanged via "
"UNIX domain sockets or other IPC mechanisms)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:61
msgid "network communications within the same or with other systems"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:63
msgid "**Capability-based protection**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:65
msgid ""
"users not having elevated capabilities (including but not limited to "
"CAP_SYS_ADMIN) may not alter the kernel's configuration, memory nor state, "
"change other users' view of the file system layout, grant any user "
"capabilities they do not have, nor affect the system's availability "
"(shutdown, reboot, panic, hang, or making the system unresponsive via "
"unbounded resource exhaustion)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:71
msgid ""
"users not having the ``CAP_NET_ADMIN`` capability may not alter the network "
"configuration, intercept nor spoof network communications from other users "
"nor systems."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:74
msgid ""
"users not having ``CAP_SYS_PTRACE`` may not observe other users' processes "
"activities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:77
msgid ""
"When ``CONFIG_USER_NS`` is set, the kernel also permits unprivileged users "
"to create their own user namespace in which they have all capabilities, but "
"with a number of restrictions (they may not perform actions that have "
"impacts on the initial user namespace, such as changing time, loading "
"modules or mounting block devices). Please refer to ``user_namespaces(7)`` "
"for more details, the possibilities of user namespaces are not covered in "
"this document."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:84
msgid ""
"The kernel also offers a lot of troubleshooting and debugging facilities, "
"which can constitute attack vectors when placed in wrong hands. While some "
"of them are designed to be accessible to regular local users with a low risk "
"(e.g. kernel logs via ``/proc/kmsg``), some would expose enough information "
"to represent a risk in most places and the decision to expose them is under "
"the administrator's responsibility (perf events, traces), and others are not "
"designed to be accessed by non-privileged users (e.g. debugfs). Access to "
"these facilities by a user who has been explicitly granted permission by an "
"administrator does not constitute a security breach."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:94
msgid ""
"Bugs that permit to violate the principles above constitute security "
"breaches. However, bugs that permit one violation only once another one was "
"already achieved are only weaknesses. The kernel applies a number of self-"
"protection measures whose purpose is to avoid crossing a security boundary "
"when certain classes of bugs are found, but a failure of these extra "
"protections do not constitute a vulnerability alone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:102
msgid "What does not constitute a security bug"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:104
msgid ""
"In the Linux kernel's threat model, the following classes of problems are "
"**NOT** considered as Linux Kernel security bugs. However, when it is "
"believed that the kernel could do better, they should be reported, so that "
"they can be reviewed and fixed where reasonably possible, but they will be "
"handled as any regular bug:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:110
msgid "**Configuration**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:112
msgid ""
"outdated kernels and particularly end-of-life branches are out of the scope "
"of the kernel's threat model: administrators are responsible for keeping "
"their system up to date. For a bug to qualify as a security bug, it must be "
"demonstrated that it affects actively maintained versions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:117
msgid ""
"build-level: changes to the kernel configuration that are explicitly "
"documented as lowering the security level (e.g. ``CONFIG_NOMMU``), or "
"targeted at developers only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:121
msgid ""
"OS-level: changes to command line parameters, sysctls, filesystem "
"permissions, user capabilities, exposure of privileged interfaces, that "
"explicitly increase exposure by either offering non-default access to "
"unprivileged users, or reduce the kernel's ability to enforce some "
"protections or mitigations. Example: write access to procfs or debugfs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:127
msgid ""
"issues triggered only when using features intended for development or "
"debugging (e.g., LOCKDEP, KASAN, FAULT_INJECTION): these features are known "
"to introduce overhead and potential instability and are not intended for "
"production use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:132
msgid ""
"issues affecting drivers exposed under CONFIG_STAGING, as well as features "
"marked EXPERIMENTAL in the configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:135
msgid ""
"loading of explicitly insecure/broken/staging modules, and generally any "
"using any subsystem marked as experimental or not intended for production "
"use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:139
msgid ""
"running out-of-tree modules or unofficial kernel forks; these should be "
"reported to the relevant vendor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:142
msgid "**Excess of initial privileges**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:144
msgid ""
"actions performed by a user already possessing the privileges required to "
"perform that action or modify that state (e.g. ``CAP_SYS_ADMIN``, "
"``CAP_NET_ADMIN``, ``CAP_SYS_RAWIO``, ``CAP_SYS_MODULE`` with no further "
"boundary being crossed)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:149
msgid ""
"actions performed in user namespace that do not bypass the restrictions "
"imposed to the initial user (e.g. ptrace usage, signal delivery, resource "
"usage, access to FS/device/sysctl/memory, network binding, system/network "
"configuration etc)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:154
msgid ""
"anything performed by the root user in the initial namespace (e.g. kernel "
"oops when writing to a privileged device)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:157
msgid "**Out of production use**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:159
msgid ""
"This covers theoretical/probabilistic attacks that rely on laboratory "
"conditions with zero system noise, or those requiring an unrealistic number "
"of attempts (e.g., billions of trials) that would be detected by standard "
"system monitoring long before success, such as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:164
msgid ""
"prediction of random numbers that only works in a totally silent environment "
"(such as IP ID, TCP ports or sequence numbers that can only be guessed in a "
"lab)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:168
msgid ""
"activity observation and information leaks based on probabilistic approaches "
"that are prone to measurement noise and not realistically reproducible on a "
"production system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:172
msgid ""
"issues that can only be triggered by heavy attacks (e.g. brute force) whose "
"impact on the system makes it unlikely or impossible to remain undetected "
"before they succeed (e.g. consuming all memory before succeeding)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:176
msgid ""
"problems seen only under development simulators, emulators, or combinations "
"that do not exist on real systems at the time of reporting (issues involving "
"tens of millions of threads, tens of thousands of CPUs, unrealistic CPU "
"frequencies, RAM sizes or disk capacities, network speeds)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:181
msgid ""
"issues whose reproduction requires hardware modification or emulation, "
"including fake USB devices that pretend to be another one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:184
msgid ""
"as well as issues that can be triggered at a cost that is orders of "
"magnitude higher than the expected benefits (e.g. fully functional keyboard "
"emulator only to retrieve 7 uninitialized bytes in a structure, or brute-"
"force method involving millions of connection attempts to guess a port "
"number)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:190
msgid "**Hardening failures**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:192
msgid ""
"ability to bypass some of the kernel's hardening measures with no "
"demonstrable exploit path (e.g. ASLR bypass, events timing or probing with "
"no demonstrable consequence). These are just weaknesses, not vulnerabilities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:197
msgid ""
"missing argument checks and failure to report certain errors with no "
"immediate consequence."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:200
msgid "**Random information leaks**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:202
msgid ""
"This concerns information leaks of small data parts that happen to be there "
"and that cannot be chosen by the attacker, or face access restrictions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:205
msgid "structure padding reported by syscalls or other interfaces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:207
msgid ""
"identifiers, partial data, non-terminated strings reported in error messages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:210
msgid ""
"Leaks of kernel memory addresses/pointers do not constitute an immediately "
"exploitable vector and are not security bugs, though they must be reported "
"and fixed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:214
msgid "**Crafted file system images**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:216
msgid ""
"bugs triggered by mounting a corrupted or maliciously crafted file system "
"image are generally not security bugs, as the kernel assumes the underlying "
"storage media is under the administrator's control, unless the filesystem "
"driver is specifically documented as being hardened against untrusted media."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:221
msgid ""
"issues that are resolved, mitigated, or detected by running a filesystem "
"consistency check (fsck) on the image prior to mounting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:224
msgid "**Physical access**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:226
msgid ""
"Issues that require physical access to the machine, hardware modification, "
"or the use of specialized hardware (e.g., logic analyzers, DMA-attack tools "
"over PCI-E/Thunderbolt) are out of scope unless the system is explicitly "
"configured with technologies meant to defend against such attacks (e.g. "
"IOMMU)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:232
msgid "**Functional and performance regressions**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../process/threat-model.rst:234
msgid ""
"Any issue that can be mitigated by setting proper permissions and limits "
"doesn't qualify as a security bug."
msgstr ""
